= Bristol Meets With Staal

Lyle Bristol, former Minister-President of Heartland and leader of our reformist wing, has—without informing the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leadership—met privately with Chancellor Staal. Bristol proposed cooperation, on the condition that Staal relinquish federal control over Heartland and restore him to office with full authority, including the power to dissolve the state Assembly. Bristol suggested that, together, they could rule by decree and wage a determined campaign against the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>.

Staal, however, was unenthusiastic. Having led the Heartland coup himself, he is reluctant to undo the federal consolidation of power. And he has no stomach for Bristol's proposed campaign against the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>—not from any love of the Crown, but because a war on the right is precisely the civil bloodletting he has set himself to prevent. He would sooner fold the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> into the state than fight them, admiring the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>’s militarism and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>’s fanatical discipline as traits to be co-opted rather than crushed. The only uprising he would meet with the full weight of the army is a <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivist</span> one—and for all his vigilance, that rising shows no sign of coming.

